// A guard on a match over an enum whose cases ALL carry no payload should
// compile and run — the guard just routes the match to the general pattern
// path, and the arms are ordinary variant patterns.
//
// It was an internal compiler error instead: the general path read a variant's
// tag with `extract_value(value, 0)` (codegen/match.py `_match_enum_pattern`),
// which assumes the `{tag, payload}` shape. An all-payload-free enum lowers to
// a bare `i32`, so the read died with `Can't index at [0] in i32`. A tuple
// scrutinee reaches the same place (`match (c, n) { case (Red, 0) -> ... }`),
// and adding a payload to ANY case of the enum made both spellings work —
// which is why examples/match_enum_guard.saw never caught it.
//
// FIXED (DF-198a) by giving the general path the shape test its sibling has had
// all along: a payload-free enum IS its tag, so there is nothing to index. The
// same landing narrowed the path's enum-name fallback, which mattered more once
// payload-free enums could reach it — every one of them is a bare integer of
// the same width, so a scan by LLVM shape alone picks an arbitrary one. It
// consults the enclosing extension's own instantiation first (as the classic
// path does) and then requires the candidate to HAVE the variant.
// See `match_payload_free_enum_general_path.saw` for the route matrix.
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// warm
// cool
// other

enum Color { case Red, case Green, case Blue }

func feel(c: Color, bright: Bool) -> String {
    match c {
        case Red if bright -> "warm",
        case Green -> "cool",
        case _ -> "other"
    }
}

func main() {
    print(feel(Color.Red, true))
    print(feel(Color.Green, true))
    print(feel(Color.Blue, true))
}
